{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-11-949-fall-2003", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["11.949"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Cities in Conflict: Theory and Practice", "course_slug": "11-949-cities-in-conflict-theory-and-practice-fall-2003", "term": "Fall", "year": 2003, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2003", "language": "en", "departments": ["Urban Studies and Planning"], "academic_levels": ["Graduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Diane Davis", "Prof. Roger Petersen"], "subject_primary": "Philosophy", "subject_secondary": ["Political Philosophy", "Social Science", "Public Administration", "Public Policy", "Regional Politics", "Sociology", "Social Justice", "Urban Studies"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Humanities", "subtopics": [{"name": "Philosophy", "specialties": ["Political Philosophy"]}]}, {"name": "Social Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Public Administration", "specialties": ["Public Policy", "Regional Politics"]}, {"name": "Sociology", "specialties": ["Social Justice"]}, {"name": "Urban Studies", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Written Assignments with Examples"], "description": "This course’s aims are two-fold: to offer students the theoretical and practical tools to understand how and why cities become torn by ethnic, religious, racial, nationalist, and/or other forms of identity that end up leading to conflict, violence, inequality, and social injustice; and to use this knowledge and insight in the search for solutions As preparation, students will be required to become familiar with social and political theories of the city and the nation and their relationship to each other. They also will focus on the ways that racial, ethnic, religious, nationalist or other identities grow and manifest themselves in cities or other territorial levels of determination (including the regional o